          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "How Long Does Concrete Sealer Last",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/how-long-does-concrete-sealer-last/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does concrete driveway sealer last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A penetrating silane-siloxane sealer lasts five to ten years on a residential driveway, shorter in a climate with heavy salting and frequent freeze-thaw. A film-forming acrylic lasts two to four years. Both figures assume a properly cleaned slab and application at the specified coverage rate in suitable weather."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I know if my concrete sealer has worn off?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Pour a cup of water on a clean, dry, trafficked area. If it beads and sits, the sealer is working. If it spreads and darkens the concrete within a minute or two, that area is unprotected. Test several spots — sealers wear out in the traffic lane long before the edges."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does concrete sealer really last 25 years?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The warranty often runs that long, but it usually covers peeling and delamination rather than water repellency. Penetrating sealers cannot peel because there is no film, so the long warranty costs the manufacturer little. Expect five to ten years of actual repellency on an exterior driveway."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should you reseal a concrete driveway?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Test every spring from about year four and reseal when two or more test areas stop repelling water — in practice every five to eight years for a penetrating sealer in a cold climate, and every eight to ten in a mild one. Acrylics need recoating every two to three years."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What makes concrete sealer fail early?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "De-icing salt, ultraviolet exposure, tyre scrub, and poor prep. Of those, prep is the one fully in your control: sealing a slab that was not properly cleaned and dried produces patchy failure within a year or two regardless of how good the product was."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does sealer last longer on new or old concrete?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "New, properly cured concrete holds a sealer longer, because the pore structure is intact and the surface paste has not weathered away. An old, open slab absorbs more product and typically gets two to three years less from the same application, though sealing it is still worthwhile."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I just add another coat when it wears out?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "With penetrating sealers, yes — clean the slab and apply a refresher coat. With film-forming acrylics you can recoat a few times, but each layer adds thickness, and once build-up starts trapping moisture or clouding, the film has to be stripped before anything new goes on."
          }
        }
