    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A sealer warranty is a statement about the product in a laboratory. What your driveway gets is sun, road salt, tyre scrub and thirty freeze-thaw cycles a year, and those decide the real number.
        </p>

        <div id="ranges" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Lifespan by Chemistry, Honestly Stated
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete sealer is not one product. The lifespan gap between the cheapest acrylic and a quality silane-siloxane is not marginal — it is a factor of five, and it is almost entirely a function of where the sealer sits. Anything that forms a film on top of the slab is exposed to everything that crosses the slab. Anything that reacts inside the pores is protected by the concrete itself.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sealer Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Realistic Exterior Life</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Warranty Claim</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Silane-siloxane penetrating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-10 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-25 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Silane only, high solids</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7-12 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15-25 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Silicate densifier</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Life of slab (no water repellency)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Permanent</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water-based acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solvent-based acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-4 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Epoxy (garage, indoors)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-10 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime kit claims</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Polyaspartic / polyurea</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-20 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15-lifetime</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The single most useful line in that table is the densifier row. Sodium and lithium silicates react with free lime in the concrete to harden the surface permanently, and that hardening genuinely does last the life of the slab. What it does not do is repel water or salt, which is why densified floors in commercial buildings are still topped with a repellent. If a product promises permanence, check which of those two jobs it is actually doing.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a residential driveway or patio, the practical choice is between two to four years of acrylic and five to ten years of penetrating sealer. The acrylic looks better while it is new and turns visibly shabby as it goes; the penetrating sealer never looks like anything at all and simply stops working quietly. Our <a href='/acrylic-vs-penetrating-concrete-sealer/'>acrylic versus penetrating comparison</a> covers which one suits which slab.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Real Life Falls Short of the Warranty
          </h2>

          <p>
            Manufacturer lifespan claims are not dishonest so much as narrowly defined. They are usually derived from accelerated weathering tests on laboratory panels: dense, correctly cured concrete, sealed under ideal conditions at the specified rate, then cycled under UV and moisture. Every one of those conditions is better than what happens on a Saturday in a real driveway.
          </p>

          <p>
            The warranty also frequently covers a specific failure mode rather than performance in general. A 25-year warranty on a penetrating sealer may guarantee that the product will not peel, blister or delaminate — which is trivially easy for a sealer that has soaked into the slab, since there is no film to peel. It says nothing about how long the water repellency lasts, and repellency is the thing you bought.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">READ THE WARRANTY CLOSELY</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A 25-year warranty on a penetrating sealer usually guarantees it will not peel — not that it will still repel water.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Those are different claims. Penetrating sealers cannot peel by design, so the long warranty is nearly free to offer. The performance figure that matters is how long the slab keeps beading water, and that is typically five to ten years on a trafficked driveway.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Application makes up much of the remaining gap. Lab panels are sealed at exactly the specified coverage rate onto a properly profiled surface. Homeowners routinely stretch product to save a container, seal a slab that was never fully cleaned, or apply on a day that is too hot. Any of those can halve the service life of an otherwise excellent product.
          </p>

          <p>
            The last piece is climate. A driveway in a mild coastal climate genuinely can hold a penetrating sealer for a decade. The same product on the same slab in a region with forty freeze-thaw cycles and heavy winter salting will be asking for attention in five or six years. Both results are normal, and neither is a product failure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="factors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Actually Shortens Sealer Life
          </h2>

          <p>
            De-icing salt is the leading cause of early sealer failure in cold climates, and magnesium and calcium chloride are worse than rock salt. They hold moisture against the surface, drive chloride into the pore structure, and cycle the slab through wetting and drying far more often than weather alone would. A driveway that gets salted every storm loses two to three years off any sealer's life compared with one that gets plowed and sanded. Our guide to the <a href='/best-ice-melt-for-concrete/'>best ice melt for concrete</a> explains which products are least punishing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ultraviolet light is the second, and it falls hardest on film-forming acrylics. UV breaks down the polymer chains in the film, which is why a south-facing patio chalks and dulls a year before a shaded one. Solvent-based acrylics generally hold colour longer than water-based; penetrating sealers are effectively immune because the film that UV would attack does not exist.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tyre traffic does most of the mechanical damage. Power steering scrub in the turning area, and hot tyres pulling at a film sealer on a summer afternoon, concentrate wear into a few square yards. This is why driveways almost never fail evenly — the strip in front of the garage gives up while the edges still bead water. If you have seen the same pattern on a coated garage floor, it is the effect described in our notes on <a href='/hot-tire-pickup-epoxy-garage-floor/'>hot tyre pickup</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, prep quality shows up years later. A sealer applied over a dusty or previously sealed surface never bonds or penetrates properly, and it fails early in patches rather than uniformly. Patchy failure within the first two years is nearly always a prep problem, not a product problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="test" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Testing Whether Yours Is Still Working
          </h2>

          <p>
            The water drop test settles it in two minutes and costs nothing. Pick a clean, dry area of the slab that gets normal traffic, pour a cup of water onto it, and watch. Water that beads up and sits proud means the sealer is still repelling. Water that spreads and darkens the concrete within a minute or two means the sealer is gone in that spot, whatever the calendar says.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test in several places, because sealers fail unevenly. Check the parking area, the turning circle, the apron at the street, the shaded edge and somewhere under the overhang if you have one. It is entirely normal to find the edges still beading while the traffic lane has failed completely, and that pattern tells you the sealer did its job and simply wore out where it was worked hardest.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Reading the Water Drop Test</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Beads up</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sealer intact, no action</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Sits flat</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Wearing, plan next season</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Darkens <2 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Gone, reseal this year</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Darkens instantly</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Overdue, check for damage</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For film-forming sealers there is a second check worth doing. Rub a rag with a little acetone on it over a small area out of sight. If the surface goes soft and tacky, you have a solvent-based acrylic still present. If nothing happens, you either have a penetrating sealer, an epoxy or nothing at all — and the water test will tell you which of those it is.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do the test in spring rather than autumn. Spring tells you what the winter did and gives you a full season of workable weather to respond in; autumn testing tends to produce a reseal rushed into weather that is too cold for the product to cure properly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Visible Signs the Sealer Has Failed
          </h2>

          <p>
            Film-forming sealers announce their failure. A glossy patio that has gone patchy and dull, a driveway with flaking white edges where the film is lifting, or a surface that looks cloudy in the morning and clears by midday are all films at the end of their lives. Once a film has begun to lift anywhere, the only durable route forward is to strip it rather than seal over it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Penetrating sealers give almost no visual warning, which is why the water test matters. The first real sign is usually darkening: the slab starts holding rain and taking longer to dry out than it used to, and puddles leave a shadow for hours. Stains that used to wipe off begin to set instead, and in cold climates the surface starts showing fine crazing as water gets in and freezes.
          </p>

          <p>
            White haze is its own category and does not always mean the sealer has failed. It can be efflorescence pushing through from below, moisture trapped under a film, or blushing from applying too thick. Our guide on <a href='/why-is-my-concrete-sealer-turning-white/'>why concrete sealer turns white</a> sorts the four causes apart, because three of them are fixable without stripping anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spalling — the surface flaking away in coin-sized chips — means the sealer failed some time ago and freeze-thaw has been working on the slab since. Resealing at that point locks the damage in. The repair has to come first, and if the spalling covers a significant share of the slab, resurfacing rather than sealing is the honest answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="extend" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Getting More Years Out of It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Prep is the cheapest lifespan you can buy. A slab that has been properly cleaned, degreased and allowed to dry completely before sealing will hold its sealer years longer than one that was hosed off on the morning of. For a driveway that means pressure washing, letting it dry for at least 24 hours in good weather, and dealing with oil stains rather than sealing over them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Apply at the right rate and in the right conditions. Most sealers want a surface between about 50 and 90 degrees Fahrenheit, out of direct midday sun, with no rain for 24 hours afterwards and preferably longer. Sealing a hot slab flashes the carrier off before the product can penetrate; sealing a cold or damp one prevents a proper cure. Neither mistake announces itself on the day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Manage the salt. Shovelling promptly, using sand for traction, and rinsing the driveway in a spring thaw all reduce chloride exposure meaningfully. If you must use a de-icer, avoid magnesium chloride on concrete under five years old and keep it off freshly sealed surfaces for the first winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance coats beat full resealing. Catching a penetrating sealer at year six with a light refresher coat, while the slab still beads water in most places, takes an afternoon and one pail. Waiting until year nine, when the surface is open and stained, means cleaning, stain removal and a full application — and the stains you did not remove are now permanent.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="schedule" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. A Schedule Worth Actually Following
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a penetrating silane-siloxane on a residential driveway in a cold climate, plan on a water test every spring from year four, and a refresher coat when two or more test spots stop beading — usually somewhere between years five and eight. In a mild climate, start testing at year six and expect to reseal around year eight to ten.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a film-forming acrylic on a patio or decorative surface, plan on recoating every two to three years, and accept from the start that at some point the accumulated film will need stripping rather than adding to. Three or four recoats is usually the practical limit before build-up starts trapping moisture and clouding.
          </p>

          <p>
            For an epoxy garage floor, expect five to ten years from a quality two-part system installed over properly profiled concrete, less from a big-box kit with insufficient prep. The failure mode is chipping and hot tyre pickup rather than gradual wear, and the fix is spot repair and a fresh topcoat rather than a full strip, provided the bulk of the coating is still bonded.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever the surface, write the date on the container and put a note in the calendar for four springs later. The reason sealers get left too long is almost never cost — it is that nobody remembers when the last one went on, and a slab that looks fine from the porch gets the benefit of the doubt for another year. The water test takes two minutes and removes the guesswork.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the Warranty Number as a Lifespan</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 25-year warranty on a penetrating sealer typically guarantees it will not peel — something it cannot do anyway. Water repellency, the property you actually bought, lasts five to ten years on a trafficked driveway. Plan around performance, not the marketing figure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging From the Porch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Penetrating sealers give no visual warning before they stop working, and a driveway can look identical the year before and the year after failure. The water drop test costs two minutes and is the only reliable check.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Recoating a Film Forever</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Each acrylic recoat adds film thickness, and after three or four the build-up starts trapping moisture and clouding. At that point the answer is stripping and starting again, not another coat over the top.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Over Spalling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Once the surface is flaking, sealer locks the damage in and makes it permanent. Repair or resurface first. A sealer is preventative; it has never repaired anything.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Resealing in the Wrong Weather</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An autumn panic reseal applied to a cold, damp slab never cures properly and can fail within a single winter. Test in spring, reseal in settled mild weather, and give the product the 24 rain-free hours it asks for.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does concrete driveway sealer last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A penetrating silane-siloxane sealer lasts five to ten years on a residential driveway, shorter in a climate with heavy salting and frequent freeze-thaw. A film-forming acrylic lasts two to four years. Both figures assume a properly cleaned slab and application at the specified coverage rate in suitable weather.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my concrete sealer has worn off?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pour a cup of water on a clean, dry, trafficked area. If it beads and sits, the sealer is working. If it spreads and darkens the concrete within a minute or two, that area is unprotected. Test several spots — sealers wear out in the traffic lane long before the edges.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does concrete sealer really last 25 years?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The warranty often runs that long, but it usually covers peeling and delamination rather than water repellency. Penetrating sealers cannot peel because there is no film, so the long warranty costs the manufacturer little. Expect five to ten years of actual repellency on an exterior driveway.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should you reseal a concrete driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Test every spring from about year four and reseal when two or more test areas stop repelling water — in practice every five to eight years for a penetrating sealer in a cold climate, and every eight to ten in a mild one. Acrylics need recoating every two to three years.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What makes concrete sealer fail early?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                De-icing salt, ultraviolet exposure, tyre scrub, and poor prep. Of those, prep is the one fully in your control: sealing a slab that was not properly cleaned and dried produces patchy failure within a year or two regardless of how good the product was.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does sealer last longer on new or old concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                New, properly cured concrete holds a sealer longer, because the pore structure is intact and the surface paste has not weathered away. An old, open slab absorbs more product and typically gets two to three years less from the same application, though sealing it is still worthwhile.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I just add another coat when it wears out?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With penetrating sealers, yes — clean the slab and apply a refresher coat. With film-forming acrylics you can recoat a few times, but each layer adds thickness, and once build-up starts trapping moisture or clouding, the film has to be stripped before anything new goes on.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

